  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 334 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nAn active period with brief moments of respite will continue this \nweek. \n\nSaturday Afternoon and Evening... Saturday afternoon and evening \nwill be one such brief moment of respite from the constant onslaught \nof rain this week. The primary concern would be the need for any \nheat headlines as heat and humidity remain in place. The \nprimary area to watch would be along and south of the HWY 34 \ncorridor, where HREF probabilities of greater than 95 degree \nheat indices are 50-90% from north to south. The NBM, which has \nhad a running warm bias, brings these probabilities (50-90%) \nfurther north to along the HWY 30 corridor. As such, depending \non the evolution of storms through Saturday morning, a heat \nheadline may be needed.\n\nSaturday night through Tuesday... Another wave moves through late \nSaturday night through Sunday morning, bringing yet another round of \nrain to the area. Severe risk is unlikely due to low bulk shear, but \nthere will be enough instability to support embedded thunderstorms. \nRainfall totals will be on the lower side, as seen by NBM \nprobabilities of 30-50% of exceeding 0.10 inches. CAMs do hint that \nlocally higher amounts will be possible wherever embedded \nthunderstorms occur. Behind these showers, a cold front will bring \ncooler temperatures to the region. These will last through midweek \nas we remain under the influence of surface high pressure. \n\nWednesday through Friday... By midweek, midlevel flow will \ntransition from zonal to northwesterly flow. This will help \nsustain the cooler temperatures established earlier in the week.\nAdditionally, signals are in place for waves to move through \nthe flow, bringing chances for more active weather.",
